Verdict
The self.de.read() reports are all FPs. I would classify the entire group as one repeated false-positive cluster.
Rudra appears to miss this real bug while repeatedly reporting the deliberate self.de.read() workaround.
Why every self.de.read() gets reported
All the warnings originate from the same structure:
de: *const &'a mut BinaryReaderDeserializer<...>
followed by:
unsafe { self.de.read() }
Rudra sees ptr::read applied to a pointer whose pointee is non-Copy—here, &mut T. It therefore marks the result as a “strong lifetime bypass.” It then treats unresolved generic calls such as Visitor::visit_*, DeserializeSeed::deserialize, BinaryFlavor::visit_f32, and Encoding::decode as possible user-controlled panic points. This is explicitly how Rudra's analysis works.
Rudra only excludes ptr::read when its pointee type is Copy; &mut T is not Copy, so every such read becomes a source.
That produces warnings in every containing generic function, as seen throughout your log.
Why the Rudra panic argument does not apply
The bug pattern Rudra is looking for is approximately:
let duplicate = ptr::read(ptr_to_owned_value);
generic_user_code_that_may_panic();
// expected ownership restoration never happens
This is dangerous for types such as:
Box<T>
Vec<T>
String
ManuallyDrop<T>
because a panic can leave two owning instances that later double-drop.
Here the copied value is:
&mut BinaryReaderDeserializer<...>
An &mut T:
- owns no allocation;
- has no destructor;
- performs no cleanup when dropped;
- cannot double-free the deserializer merely because the generic call panics.
So Rudra's flow:
ptr::read(non-Copy) → unresolved generic call
exists syntactically, but its intended consequence:
duplicated owner → panic → double-drop / corrupted ownership
does not.
This code was deliberately introduced as a Miri fix
The pointer-based implementation was introduced by upstream PR #150, titled “Use pointers in token reader deserializations.” Its stated purpose was to avoid invalidating references and to re-enable Miri's stacked-borrows checking.
The changelog for v0.25.2 says the remaining soundness issues detected by Miri were fixed; v0.27.0 was released later and contains that corrected pointer implementation.
